{
  "scope": "The sessions in scope run from corpus.first_session to source.last_session in search/config.yaml; sessions_in_scope gives the first and the last. An older speech is in scope when its protocol belongs to one of them, and the protocol's session is read from its id. Older protocols of other sessions lie outside the sessions the corpus covers and are counted in old_corpus.out_of_scope. Coverage is the share of the older corpus's in-scope speeches that are paired with a speech in the new corpus. The reverse share counts the new corpus's speeches dated from the first to the last date of the older corpus's in-scope protocols, and the share of them paired with an older speech.",
  "pairing": "Pairs are made only within one protocol and one party, and only between slugs that agree on the speaker's name. Two slugs that both end in a place must name the same place. A slug that ends in a place agrees with one that does not only when the two, the place left out, are at least 0.95 alike, letter by letter. Otherwise the given name and surname that end the new slug must be found among the older slug's tokens, or the two names must be at least 0.95 alike. Two members of one party with the same name agree where neither slug gives a place, or where one gives it and the other holds the name alone. A pair needs at least min_shared_tokens shared tokens, and at least min_sequence_share of the new speech's pairs of adjacent tokens must be found in the older text. The test runs in that direction only. A new speech that has lost words can therefore pair, and coverage does not show whether the new speeches are complete. The other direction is described by overlap.old_in_new and by the figures under surplus, which count what the older text holds beyond the new speech. Where both corpora carry the same words, the reconciliation cannot tell the record's text from the speaker's. That is why the paragraphs set aside as the record's own are checked by reading them when the corpus is built.",
